===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{senseid|en|Q334600}} [[repetition|Repetition]] of an [[activity]] to improve a [[skill]].
#: {{syn|en|practicing<id:Q334600>|drill<id:Q334600>|exercise<id:Q334600>}}
#: {{nearsyn|en|q1=sometimes synonymous|training#Noun|rehearsing#Noun|rehearsal#Noun|dry run#Noun|trial#Noun|workout#Noun}}
#: {{ux|en|He will need lots of '''practice''' with the lines before he performs them.}}
## An organized event for the purpose of performing such repetition.
##: {{ux|en|Being on a team is hard: you're always having to go to '''practice''' while everyone else is taking it easy.}}
##: {{ux|en|I have choir '''practice''' every Sunday after church.}}
## The state of being proficient due to repetition of a skill.
##: {{ux|en|I was rusty, but after a few months, I got back into '''practice'''.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|especially|medicine|arts}} The ongoing pursuit of a [[craft]] or [[profession]], particularly in medicine or the fine arts.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=Raphael Vella|title=Artist-Teachers in Context: International Dialogues|publisher=Springer|isbn=9789463006330|page=53
|passage=Which is the most demanding? I think that my '''practice''' as an artist is 'stronger' because it is the '''practice''' that best fuels and balances myself and that generates new knowledge for my other work as both arts educator and creative arts therapist.}}
## Skilful or artful management; dexterity in contrivance or the use of means; stratagem; artifice.
##* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|passage=He sought to have that by '''practice''' which he could not by prayer.}}
## {{lb|en|math}} An easy and concise method of applying the rules of [[arithmetic]] to questions which occur in trade and business.
## {{lb|en|religion}} The observance of [[religious]] duties that an spiritual institution requires of its members.
## {{lb|en|legal}} The form, manner, and order of conducting and carrying on [[suit]]s and [[prosecution]]s through their various stages, according to the principles of law and the rules laid down by the courts.
##: {{ux|en|This firm of solicitors is involved in family law '''practice'''.}}
## {{lb|en|countable}} A place where a [[professional service]] is provided, such as a [[general practice]].
##: {{syn|en|general practice}}
##: {{ux|en|She ran a thriving medical '''practice'''.}}
# Actual operation or [[experiment]], in contrast to [[theory]].
#: {{ant|en|theory}}
#: {{ux|en|That may work in theory, but will it work in '''practice'''?}}
## A [[customary]] [[action]], [[habit]], or [[behaviour]]; a [[manner]] or [[routine]].
##: {{syn|en|custom|habit|pattern|routine|wont|wone}}
##: {{ux|en|It is the usual '''practice''' of employees there to wear neckties only when meeting with customers.}}
##: {{ux|en|It is good '''practice''' to check each door and window before leaving.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1954|month=August|author=J. B. Snell|title=The New Zealand Government Railways—2|journal=Railway Magazine|page=558|text=As a result, New Zealand locomotive design is a curious blend of English and American '''practice'''; certainly the American influence has always been much stronger than in Australia.}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|title=Balearic islands pass bill targeting boozy Brits abroad|date=2020-01-17|author=Stephen Burgen|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2020/jan/17/balearic-islands-bill-alcohol-fuelled-holidays|passage=The legislation also seeks to outlaw “balconing”, the '''practice''' of jumping into a swimming pool from a hotel balcony that kills several people each year. In future, hotels will have to evict anyone practising balconing or encouraging others to do so.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20200320085630/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2020/jan/17/balearic-islands-bill-alcohol-fuelled-holidays}}

====Usage notes====
* British, Australian, and New Zealand English spelling distinguishes between {{m|en|practice|pos=noun}} and {{m|en|practise|pos=verb}}, analogously with {{m|en|advice}}/{{m|en|advise}}. In American English, the spelling {{m|en|practice}} is commonly used for both noun and verb. Both practices are found equally in Canadian English.

